WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.510
(pensive music)

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:03.490
- [Narrator] Throughout the pandemic,

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.580
athletes have been the
most tested population

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.210
on the planet, sometimes
providing examples

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.800
of emerging theories about COVID-19.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.520
And as countries around the world continue

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.350
to fight back COVID-19,

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.160
attention has turned to Tokyo

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.070
where the Olympics are under way.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.825
One challenge some athletes are facing:

00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:21.830
testing positive for the virus,

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.710
despite having been vaccinated.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.270
This isn't the first we've heard of this.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.424
- What are chances of getting COVID-19

00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:31.030
after receiving a vaccine?

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.640
Well, you've got a better chance

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.700
of buying a winning lottery ticket

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.900
but it happened to
Warriors player Damion Lee.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.010
- [Reporter 1] He will be
out at least two weeks.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.390
It was not a false
positive as first thought,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.730
even though Walman had been vaccinated.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.010
- [Reporter 2] Yankees
GM Brian Cashman says

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.870
that three players tested positive

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.420
and three others are likely infected.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.410
About 85% of the team is vaccinated,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.920
including those who have the virus.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.100
- [Narrator] These examples are known

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.500
as breakthrough infections,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.170
which occur when someone tests positive

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.740
for COVID-19 at least
two weeks after getting

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.210
their final shot.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.160
Do these breakthrough cases
mean the vaccines are failing?

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.250
- No, the vaccines are not failing.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.910
The vaccines are working extremely well,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.650
and as expected.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.190
They do protect the majority of recipients

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.080
from severe disease.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.490
- [Narrator] That's what
vaccines are designed to do:

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.960
Prevent death and severe disease.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.150
But most vaccines, including those created

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.800
to fight COVID-19

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.360
don't completely protect
you from infection.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.890
So it's not all that surprising

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.417
that breakthrough
infections are showing up.

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:38.070
As of July 12th, more than
159 million people in the US

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.710
have been fully vaccinated.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.960
CDC data suggests just under 5,500

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.430
have had breakthrough infections,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.370
resulting in hospitalizations or deaths.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.420
That's one in approximately 29,000 people

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.320
who have been vaccinated.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.850
- Breakthrough infections are something

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.370
that we want to monitor

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.350
but in terms of their overall
influence in the pandemic,

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.040
they play a much smaller role

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.200
than transmission among people
who haven't been vaccinated.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.020
- [Narrator] But these
cases raise questions

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.750
about our immunity to the virus

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.310
and hint at a future in which
it isn't gone completely

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.400
and we learn to live with it.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.600
That's due in part to variants.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.170
Research shows that variants,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.540
including Delta, can partially
evade the immune response

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.070
from prior infection and vaccination.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.340
- We mount a really good immune response

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.550
against the virus that our
body's trying to recognize.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.660
Your body is really good

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.340
at recognizing and neutralizing
those specific threats.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.100
But when the virus starts to change,

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.880
sometimes it doesn't
recognize the virus as well.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.120
And so that's how you
sort of see it chip away

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.650
at that immune response.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.280
That's one of the reasons
that health officials

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.770
are really sort of concerned

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.160
about this global vaccination drive

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.590
in order to prevent the
virus from spreading,

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.900
both to save lives

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.190
and to prevent it from further mutating

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.120
and evading immune response.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.120
- [Narrator] The Delta variant

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.520
is the most contagious version

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.490
of the virus to be identified,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.730
but research suggests
that full vaccination

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.040
is still protective against
severe disease and death:

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.800
the outcomes that have made
COVID-19 so devastating.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.910
What dictates whether
someone is more likely

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.490
to get infected, even
if they're vaccinated?

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:16.180
Dr. Hatziioannou says there
are four main variables.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.570
First is the amount of
virus that is circulating

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.850
in your community.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.930
- So if a great number of
people around you are infected,

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:23.970
then the possibility

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.820
of you getting exposed
obviously increases.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.680
If you're in close proximity with people

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:29.610
that are infected,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.600
particularly those that are unvaccinated

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.270
and have generally higher viral loads,

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.230
then the probability of you
getting infected increases.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.670
- [Narrator] The second
is tied to vaccine uptake.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:43.210
- So if a large proportion of
the population is vaccinated,

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:46.880
then your virus transmission,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.760
virus loads, everything decreases.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.800
So the chances of spreading the virus

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.610
amongst this population
obviously decreases.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.330
- [Narrator] That's because
vaccinated people act

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.650
as a kind of shield,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.140
even when they do get infected.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.730
A recent CDC study found
that vaccinated people

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.520
carried less virus and
potentially didn't spread it

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.530
as much as unvaccinated people.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.963
Cases were also shorter and less severe.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.080
Vaccines help create a
kind of immune memory

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.760
of what a virus looks like,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.720
helping the body fight it off more quickly

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:15.830
when it spots it.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.750
That makes it harder for
the virus to spread overall.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.630
Roughly half of all Americans
are fully vaccinated

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.730
but in some states and globally,

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.950
the vaccination rate is much lower,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.320
giving the virus more
opportunity to spread and mutate.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.880
That's why having large gathering,

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.030
like concerts or the Olympics
can be so challenging

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:35.713
from a public health perspective.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.760
Third, individual behavior matters.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.210
- So as measures have been abandoned,

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.690
such as masking and social distancing,

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.210
when you don't have a significant number

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.400
of the population vaccinated,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.280
then the ability of the
vaccines to protect you

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.010
from getting infected decrease.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.160
- [Narrator] Finally,
even after vaccination,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.950
individual immune systems
vary in their ability

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.940
to prevent and fight off infection.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.120
Older and immunocompromised people seem

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.300
to be more susceptible
to breakthrough cases,

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.180
and those tend to be severe.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.170
- That's why health officials

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.470
are considering additional
doses right now,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.200
primarily for people who have
a compromised immune system

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.550
and might not have produced
a good immune response

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.630
after two doses of the vaccine.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.050
- [Narrator] Breakthrough
cases might be asymptomatic

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.040
or mild, so people may
not know to get tested.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.370
But that's not the case for athletes.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.630
- We're actually seeing
breakthrough infections happen

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.480
a lot more amongst sports teams,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.680
like baseball or for the Olympics

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.650
because those are the people

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.530
that get tested pretty regularly,

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.060
even if they're vaccinated.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:38.960
- [Narrator] At the Olympics,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.040
organizers of the games are scrambling

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.740
to deal with a rising load
of athletes and officials

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.520
who are testing positive
upon arrival in Japan,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.600
some with breakthrough infections.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.470
The vaccination rates are low in Japan.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:51.860
Cases there are rising

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.450
and organizers didn't
require those participating

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.030
to get vaccinated.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.850
Dr. Hatziioannou and many
other experts remain concerned

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.870
that holding a large-scale
international event prior

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.150
to reaching a critical mass

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.750
of vaccinated individuals
has the potential

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.010
to contribute to the virus's
spread around the world.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.010
- The Olympics is a special event

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.230
and it's truly remarkable

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.740
that it brings all these people

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.930
from all these different
countries together

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.280
but it also poses a perfect ground

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.930
to mix variants and spread the virus

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.223
that will then go back to
each athlete's country.

